  "Question": "Two different positive numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) each differ from their reciprocals by \\(1.\\) What is \\(a + b?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\sqrt{5}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\sqrt{6}\\)",
    "E": "\\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because the numbers are different and only their sum is requested, label the larger one \\(a\\), so \\(a>b>0\\). Differing from a reciprocal by \\(1\\) allows either signed difference, \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\).\n\nSince \\(a>b>0\\), their reciprocals satisfy \\(1/a<1/b\\). Consequently,\n\\[\na-\\frac1a>b-\\frac1b.\n\\]\nBoth expressions are either \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), so they must satisfy\n\\[\na-\\frac1a=1,\\qquad b-\\frac1b=-1.\n\\]\nAdding these equations reveals their product:\n\\[\na+b=\\frac1a+\\frac1b=\\frac{a+b}{ab}.\n\\]\nHere \\(ab>0\\) and \\(a+b>0\\), so multiplying by \\(ab\\) and dividing by \\(a+b\\) gives \\(ab=1\\). Thus \\(1/a=b\\), and the first equation becomes \\(a-b=1\\).\n\nThe product and difference now determine the sum:\n\\[\n(a+b)^2=(a-b)^2+4ab=1+4=5.\n\\]\nBecause \\(a+b\\) is positive, \\(a+b=\\boxed{\\sqrt5}\\), choice C.",
